/* Player-authored 1973 coalition event prose. Tracking notes are not event titles. */
(function(root){'use strict';const text={
chp_first:`Despite all polls and expectations, thanks to our new leadership's efforts, we have secured first place since 1961! An air of optimism has washed across our party for the prospects of the Left of the Center Movement! However, now begins the difficult task of coalition talks, for our party lacks a majority and thus will have to form a government with one of our right-wing competitors...`,
chp_cgp_intro:`Thankfully, our electoral achievements were great enough that arithmetics support a potential CHP-CGP coalition! Despite our difficult past and personal feuds, we have managed to make CGP consider such a coalition and will now enter into talks with CGP leadership.`,
chp_cgp_talks:`It was an unique day to say to least in CHP's Headquarters, once comrades turned rivals have once again sat in the same room and were drinking tea where they once all sat together; Ferit Melen, Turhan Feyzioğlu and Kemal Satır alongside our other old friends sat across us after greeting one another. Mr. Melen particularly carried a tense expression, though we were not seeing an hostility in him, thanks to our past, we knew that his primary concern was of us 'sullying the name of Kemalism with Communism' and 'straying from the baba ocağı', however, despite his anti-communist sentiments, we could still certainly have a middle ground. Mr. Feyzioğlu had a far more pleased expression, he was once the leader of the Center of the Left Movement and had personally protected Mr. Ecevit himself from being labeled as a Communist, and while our differences later on had caused in his not well thought resignation from our party, fate has brought us together once more... And Mr. Satır, the most recent high profile defector of CHP, after having founded CP, the dynamic nature of the military governments preceding Talu's caretaker government had nearly allowed him the chance to from a real party, however İnönü's refusel to join it in the end led him to enter into talks with MGP which led to formation of CGP. It was hard to make out of his expression, thanks to freshness of our conflicts that were younger than a year, the tension with him was the greatest...`,
chp_cgp_cabinet:`As the first greetings end and we make the general conclusion of what this "Kemalist Coalition" meant for our republic, it was now time to talk about the formation of our cabinet. However many ministries either party will get to have depends on our electoral performances.`,
chp_cgp_powers:`With the cabinet talks out of the way, we must now talk about the agenda and program of this new Kemalist Coalition. Luckily, CGP is just as progressive as we are in social issues, however the topics of economic democratization and the Kurdish Issue will be particular points of tension... Despite this, the atmosphere is lighthearted and optimistic, with both side seeing the other side as genuine in our aspirations for our beloved republic our Founding Father had created.`,
chp_cgp_arithmetic:`Unfortunately, our electoral achievements were not great enough that arithmetics support a potential CHP-CGP coalition. Therefore we will have to look for other alternatives... But what options do we truly have...?`,
chp_cgp_failed:`It is incredibly heartbreaking, for despite the amazing electoral achievements and the air of optimism between the 2 parties, the attempts at forming a coalition with CGP has failed... Therefore, we will have to look for other alternatives... But what options do we truly have...?`,
msp_transition:`With the prospect of a CHP-CGP coalition not a possibility, we are instead looking for potential coalition arrangements with other right wing parties, however our choices are relatively limited. Within our party's never members, Deniz Baykal, a young and energetic figure within our party that started to rise in prominence over his reports on the 1965 general elections, has come forward with his plans of a temporary coalition with the Islamist MSP...

What a prospect! A governing coalition of strictly secular CHP, and a literal Islamist Party that has already been once shut down in its former body! However, despite the incredibly apparent division between the 2 parties, the more this topic is discussed within our party, and more and more contact is being made with MSP, it is becoming annoyingly more and more possible...

Aside from the obvious clash regarding religious and cultural issues, MSP's economic program and aspirations are more similar to ours than it is with the Clientelist AP, though a sharp difference still exists with MSP's blatant stance on KOBİs, the small business sectors of our republic, Petty Bourgouise, which Unfortunately, by both preference and design, rely on cheap and unregulated labor... And finally, their new leader Erbakan and his boys are surprisingly receptive to our calls; it seems they want to break away from the fringes.

It seems like this Forced Marriage might just happen...`,
chp_msp_intro:`Having completed our discussions with CGP, we must now consider the possibility of forming a government with the National Salvation Party. However, since no proposal had previously been made to them, the MSP leadership has not yet considered such a possibility within its authorized party bodies. Whether a government can be formed between our two parties remains uncertain, but we shall have the opportunity to explain our thoughts on how a new government might be formed, and perhaps find a point of agreement despite our different ways of thinking.`,
chp_msp_talks:`At 11:00 a.m., we arrived at Room 280 of the Parliament building, accompanied by Orhan Birgit, where Mr. Erbakan received us alongside his Parliamentary Group Deputy Chairmen, Hasan Aksay and Süleyman Arif Emre. After learning that Mr. Emre would also participate in the meeting, we summoned our Secretary-General {{secretary}}. Before the discussions began, we exchanged pleasantries, inquired after each other's well-being, and spoke briefly about Mr. Erbakan's recent visit to Istanbul and the exhausting nature of election campaigns. Once the journalists had left the room, our discussions began. Over the following two hours, we explained in considerable detail our thoughts on how a government might be formed, while Mr. Erbakan listened on behalf of the MSP. As he would later explain to journalists, we had not spent those two hours discussing trivial matters when the country faced such important problems. However, the MSP leadership would first have to convey our ideas to its authorized party bodies before any further discussions could take place.`,
msp_postponement:`As coalition negotiations continue, the MSP demands that the upcoming local elections, scheduled for December 9, be postponed until May 1974. Although Erbakan cites winter conditions and the proximity of the elections as justification, it is clear that his newly established party needs more time to organize. In exchange, they offer to support our candidate for Speaker of Parliament. However, with our party fully prepared for the elections, accepting this demand is no easy decision…`,
chp_msp_cabinet:`With our initial discussions concluded, the question remains whether a government can actually be formed between our two parties. Mr. Erbakan has made it clear that what he personally thinks of our ideas is not important; what matters is how the MSP's authorized party bodies regard them. He has therefore refrained from offering a judgment that might influence or prejudice their deliberations. The MSP has designated later dates in November as the earliest possible dates for these bodies to convene, and we shall have to await their decision before proceeding any further. Nevertheless, we believe that finding a solution to the government question as quickly as possible would be beneficial and necessary in every respect. Should a coalition between our two parties prove possible, we must undertake the necessary work promptly... Eventually, the 2 sides sit one more time, with Mr. Erbakan having had conversed with MSP's young bodies and factions, we could finally make fruitful talks over allocation of this cabinet.`,
chp_msp_powers:`As our discussions turn toward the possibility of establishing a common government program, the differences between our political outlooks become apparent. Mr. Erbakan has previously stated that while we may agree on the names of the illnesses afflicting our country, agreement on their diagnosis requires agreement on their causes. He has also described the National Vision as equally distant from the leftist and liberal viewpoints, with each representing a distinct current of thought. Nevertheless, he has emphasized his desire to maintain good relations between political parties and pursue a constructive approach to the country's problems. As he explained to journalists, there are now three brothers living together in the same house, here in Parliament. These brothers do not share identical ideas, but they are all children of the same nation and wish to serve their country. What is now at issue is the arrangement of the interior of that house, and although the three brothers do not think alike, they must somehow find a point of agreement. Whether such an agreement can be reached will depend on how the advantages and disadvantages of a coalition are weighed by our respective party bodies...`,
chp_msp_arithmetic:`With our arithmetics not supporting a potential coalition, any talks with MSP would sadly bear no fruit. While it would have been a curious experiment and something much suggested by Mr Baykal from our party as a temporary solution, we sadly have to look for our final alternative...`,
chp_msp_failed:`Expectadly, talks between the Progressive CHP and Reactionary MSP failed to achieve a governing coalition. Our differences were too painfully stark that time and time again we either failed to settle on ministries or our party's programs. In the end, disappointment for failure of our labor and annoyance at ourselves for even thinking this would work were the dominant emotions within our headquarters. However, we seldom had time to grieve and hit walls as still had to find someone to lead this republic...`,
chp_ap_intro:`With all other prospects exhausted, we are only left with one final possibility for coalition building; Demirkırat's hooves knock on our doors... AP is our greatest rival in politics, achieving consecutive electoral victories in 1965 and 1969, and it was highly expected of them achieving another victory in this election. Interestingly enough, the first ever coalition in Turkish history was between our CHP and AP under its founder Ragıp Gümüşpala in the onset of 27 May, however, that coalition leaves seldom hope for a new one between our parties today... Our parties are significantly opposed to one another, in politics, in social order, in economics, and all other aspects one could think of, only point we could realistically agree and cooperate under is the end of Memorandum governments. A CHP-AP coalition will undoubtable massive in the parliament, potentially allowing us access to constitutional amendments, however due to both of us being potential governing parties, our history and differences would make such coalition highly volatile and require huge concessions from each side, nevertheless, what other option do we have beside leaving the mandate, after which it would go to AP, and we'd fine ourself as the junior partner of a Grand Coalition...`,
chp_ap_talks:`It was a very tense day in CHP Headquarters, cups of Turkish coffee letting out a warm smell with its gentle steam, however the faces in the room could hardly be compared, CHP's enthusiasm over being the largest party in the elections has diminished considerably with failures to establish a more preferable coalition as well as the exhaustion the prior talks have brought. Party Chairman Mr. Ecevit was sitting with his General Secretary {{secretary}} and Orhan Birgit, a member of Ecevit's inner circle, all 3 figures were half crouched and leaning towards the table with Ecevit's elbows resting on the table as the slight frown on his face was making his moustache cover his upper lips, on the other side of the table, the situation was not much different, albeit for different reasons...

Facing Ecevit directly was Mr. Demirel, who was resting his back against the sofa, in his hands was his cup of coffee with its saucer, only his cup was halfway drank, his expression was a mix of conflicting emotions, he was trying to exert a sense of superiority with his chin up, however, his face was half tensed at the annoyance of being the guest and not the other way around, the victory drunk man who had enjoyed electoral victories only comparable to Menderes could not accept this jumpstart taking his place... To his left and right sat AP Grand Secretary Nizamettin Erkmen and AP Senate Head Ahmet Nusret Tuna, Mr. Erkmen was a relatively moderate Conservative that was not a difficult man to work with on a personal level, but it would become apparent early on for CHP that he was more or less a yes man for Demirel, Mr. Tuna was a more well known figure, a center of authority within AP, as well as a more liberal man compared to more Demirel and Erkmen, while at the same time being more conservative than both, as someone that could be best described as a Constitutionalist Democrat, his job within the party suited him well, and through the conversations he appeared to be more independent from Demirel in comparison to Erkmen.

The pleasantries were done with faster than gentlemenship would prefer, and the back and forth almost bickering dialogue over the prospect of a coalition would be done hastily, AP actually even considering such coalition was already something no one would have thought about a year ago, and before any substantial talks could be made over a potential government, AP camp has given us a demand; Ecevit would not be the Prime Minister, but neither Demirel...

AP made it clear that blatantly did not want Ecevit as the sitting leader of this coalition, refusing to accept what they call an unequal partnership, and instead insists on a non-partisan Prime Minister with party leaders instead acting as Deputy Prime Ministers. The demand brings instant uproar from our camp, however in the sheer lack of options of alternatives the discussions proceed, as both sides contemplate over a potential option, the concluded name was Sadi Irmak.

An ex-CHP member and a member of previous Peker government, he is a relatively neutral technocratic figure that we would prefer to work under and AP would accept. With a candidate chosen for this uneasy coalition and AP waiting our answer, we must either accept it, refuse it and return the mandate to form a government and hope a better solution appearing in future, but also potentially risking a less favorable coalition with AP as the junior partner if things don't go well, as they hadn't so far...`,
chp_ap_cabinet:`Wheter we like it or not, the prime minister was chosen, and with both parties having a general understanding of what kind of coalition was awaiting us, it was now time to trade ministries between both sides. With a bipartisan Prime Minister, the value of ministries increases a lot more as at the very least ministries' decentralized structure in Turkey would allow us to achieve at least some segments of our program...`,
chp_ap_powers:`With the ministries chosen, the final topic was regarding both sides' redlines, as 2 parties that are extremely different, we have a large number of issues we disagree at one another, and it is crucial that we create an actual functioning lines both sides accept if we intend on this coalition actually lasting.`,
chp_ap_failed:`As expected, even if arithmatics made it possible, it didn't mean a coalition was bound to happen, our irreconcilable differences with AP resulted in our talks bearing no fruit, both sides leave the meeting with heavy steps and grumpy breaths as media is left yearning for proper explanations. With the last possible coalition member being spent, CHP is left with no other alternatives, it looks like it wasn't Karaoğlan's time to shine...`,
return_first:`With our failure to form a coalition government, Ecevit reluctantly resigns his mandate to form a coalition and informs the president. With our resignation, the President gives the mandate to the second largest party, AP, and it was only a matter of time before we were invited for an awkward meeting...`,
ap_invitation:`Shortly after Demirel was given the mandate the form a government, AP has sent Orhan Dengiz and Ali Naili Erdem, 2 MPs from AP to invite us to their headquarters to make coalition talks.`,
ap_invitation_refused:`We have already refused to form a government with them, why would we change our mind for a worse deal? We sent off the 2 men, let Demirel now lead with the pain of coalition building.`,
ap_second_talks:`Shortly after Demirel was given the mandate the form a government, we have expectedly been called to AP headquarters, however due to raw hostility and annoyance from our side we instead demanded to meet in a reserved room in the Parliament, to which AP eventually agreed. The pleasantries were done even faster than before, and the discussions were also done faster, though also with more visible anger from CHP's side, while Demirel's previous arrogant expression was now the only thing Ecevit could see in him, for the man was happy, very happy indeed.

For when the topic of prime ministry, CHP had expected to hear AP's potential non-partisan candidate, we were instead hit with Demirel's insistence on his own prime ministry!

The audacity!

The audacity to demand a non-partisan prime minister when we came to their door, only for them to demand it for themselves this time, even though we were the larger party! They might as well asked all 3 men to bend over and sing the national anthem as they do their business!`,
ap_second_failed:`The audacity indeed! With AP's outrageous demands, we eventually politely make our way out of the room, that was effectively as storming out. Demirel grunts to himself in annoyance and lets a breath out, as it looked like this whole ordeal was going to get a whole lot messier...`,
talu_proposal:`With the second failure of a Grand Coalition, Demirel also resigns from his mandate and informs the president. With the failure of 2 main parties of the parliament, the mandate is next given to Naim Talu, the head of the current caretaker government that was originally only supposed to lead the republic until the 1973 elections. With the new given mandate, Naim Talu first tries to make talk with all parties for his proposed technocratic government that would encompass CHP, AP and CGP, with lackluster enthusiasm from all parties, us included, Naim Talu sees the impossibility of any legitimate government passing vote of confidence, makes his final decision...`,
talu_failed:`... Talu resigns his mandate and informs the president, in less than 2 months, 3 candidates have resigned the mandate, exhaustion, annoyance and uncertainty engulfs our nation as our population also further loses belief in the parliamentary politics introduced after 27 May, a trend that has been happening since 1963... With no way out of this ordeal, it is decided to wait until the upcoming local elections and see how these events shaped the people's opinions on the ballot, perhaps this would strengthen our hand for a potential coalition... However, for the time being, Talu's existing government stays in power for the time being.`,
renewed:`At last, some good news! After months of political uncertainty and the exhausting attempts to establish a government following the general elections, the people have once again gone to the ballot boxes, this time to elect their local representatives and municipal administrations. And much to our delight, the results have proven to be an even greater victory for the Left of the Center Movement than the general elections themselves! Not only have we maintained our position as the largest party in the country, but our share of the popular vote has increased even further, demonstrating that the enthusiasm surrounding our new leadership was hardly a temporary electoral phenomenon... The people are increasingly placing their faith in our movement, and although these victories have not provided us with additional seats in the National Assembly, they have certainly strengthened our political legitimacy and, perhaps more importantly, our position in the negotiations to come!

The results have also placed the other parties in a rather uncomfortable position, for while the parliamentary arithmetic remains precisely as it was following the general elections, the political atmosphere has changed considerably. Those who previously believed that our electoral achievements might prove temporary can no longer so easily dismiss the growing popularity of Mr. Ecevit and the Center of the Left, and the prospect of refusing cooperation with the party that has now won two consecutive elections may become increasingly difficult to justify before the public... President, having observed the renewed expression of public confidence in our party and the continued inability of the existing political leadership to establish a stable government, has consequently decided to entrust Mr. Ecevit with the mandate to form a government once more. With the confidence of the electorate behind us and considerably greater leverage than during our previous attempts, we shall once again approach the National Salvation Party, whose participation remains one of the few realistic possibilities for establishing a parliamentary majority...`,
renewed_msp_intro:`With our electoral position considerably strengthened by the recent local elections, we must once again attempt to find common ground with the National Salvation Party. Our earlier discussions with Mr. Erbakan had demonstrated that, despite the considerable ideological distance separating our two parties, the possibility of cooperation could not be dismissed altogether. Indeed, the MSP leader himself had emphasized that the political parties represented different ways of thinking, but were nevertheless children of the same nation, all wishing to serve their country. Whether those sentiments can now be translated into an actual coalition agreement remains to be seen...

Nevertheless, unlike our previous encounter, we now enter these discussions with the advantage of having received a renewed expression of public confidence, and with the alternatives to a CHP-MSP government having become increasingly limited, perhaps Mr. Erbakan and his colleagues will be more willing to seriously consider the prospect of governing alongside us.

This time, however, the negotiations will be conducted by representatives appointed by the leaderships of both parties, with Mr. Ecevit entrusting Deniz Baykal, Cahit Kayra and Erol Çevikçe with the responsibility of representing our interests. The MSP, meanwhile, has appointed Oğuzhan Asiltürk, Süleyman Arif Emre and İhsan Karaçal to negotiate on its behalf. The responsibility of forming the government remains with Mr. Ecevit, but much will now depend upon whether these six men can establish a sufficiently solid foundation for their respective leaders to approve a coalition...`,
renewed_msp_talks:`It was a rather peculiar day in Ankara, one that seemed to carry both the exhaustion of the preceding months and a renewed sense of possibility... The meeting room was neither particularly grand nor remarkable, a long wooden table occupying most of its center, with the usual glasses of tea and several stacks of documents arranged before the seats. Yet the importance of what was about to take place could hardly be understated, for after months of failed attempts at forming a government, the representatives of the two parties that had emerged as the principal alternatives to the Justice Party were once again preparing to discuss the possibility of governing together. There was, however, one rather important distinction from our earlier meetings; neither Mr. Ecevit nor Mr. Erbakan would be directly participating in these negotiations, having instead entrusted the discussions to their respective representatives...

Among our members, Deniz Baykal was the prominent supporter of a potential temporary coalition with MSP and had convinced the party for the first time, the youngest-looking and perhaps most visibly enthusiastic member of our delegation, therefore appeared particularly determined to make something of this renewed opportunity. His manner was confident, and although the negotiations had barely begun, the occasional glances he exchanged with his colleagues suggested that he was already anticipating the difficult arguments that would inevitably arise.

Facing us were Oğuzhan Asiltürk, Süleyman Arif Emre and İhsan Karaçal, each of whom had been entrusted by Mr. Erbakan with the task of representing the National Salvation Party. Mr. Emre was a rather familiar figure to us, having already participated in the earlier meeting between Ecevit and Erbakan, and his familiarity with the previous discussions made him a particularly important presence at the table. He appeared intent upon keeping the conversation within the boundaries of what the MSP's authorized bodies might actually approve, occasionally intervening to clarify a point or prevent an argument from proceeding too far.

The afternoon progressed with considerable discussion, occasional disagreements and several moments in which it appeared that the ideological distance between our parties might prove too great to overcome. Nevertheless, neither delegation seemed particularly eager to abandon the negotiations. Our representatives were well aware that another failure could force Mr. Ecevit to return the mandate to the President, while the MSP's representatives could hardly ignore the possibility that such a development might ultimately bring Mr. Demirel back into government. With the alternatives becoming increasingly limited, the necessity of finding some point of agreement had become more than a mere expression of political goodwill... And so, after several hours of discussions, both delegations agreed that the possibility of a coalition merited further examination, with the next stage concerning the composition of the government itself.`,
renewed_msp_cabinet:`With our preliminary discussions having concluded, and the representatives of both parties having reported the results to their respective leaderships, we could finally proceed toward the formation of a cabinet. Mr. Ecevit, who remained responsible for the mandate entrusted to him by the President, had been informed of the progress made by Deniz Baykal, Kayra and Çevikçe, while Mr. Erbakan had likewise received the assessments of Asiltürk, Emre and Karaçal. Although the atmosphere surrounding the negotiations had become considerably more promising, the question of how the ministries would be distributed between our two parties remained unresolved, and neither delegation appeared particularly inclined to make the task easy for the other...`,
renewed_msp_powers:`With the cabinet negotiations finally concluded, we must now turn toward what may prove to be the most difficult aspect of this rather unusual coalition; the establishment of a common government program... Despite the increasingly constructive atmosphere surrounding our discussions, the ideological distance separating our Left of the Center Movement from Mr. Erbakan's National Vision remains considerable, and it would be rather foolish to imagine that the distribution of ministries has somehow resolved the fundamental disagreements between our parties. Indeed, Mr. Erbakan himself had previously offered a rather appropriate metaphor for the situation, explaining that while different political movements might agree upon the names of the illnesses afflicting our country, genuine agreement over their diagnosis requires agreement concerning their underlying causes. And herein lies the difficulty, for although both parties recognize the necessity of addressing the country's economic and social problems, our respective understandings of their origins, and consequently the measures necessary to resolve them, are hardly identical...`,
renewed_msp_failed:`It is an extraordinarily bitter disappointment... Despite our remarkable achievements in the recent local elections, the renewed confidence of the electorate and the considerable efforts of our representatives, the attempts at establishing a coalition government with the National Salvation Party have ultimately failed. Mr. Baykal, Kayra and Çevikçe have returned from the negotiations with little to show for their efforts beyond the realization that the differences between our respective political programs have proven too difficult to reconcile. On the opposite side, Asiltürk, Emre and Karaçal have likewise been unable to secure an arrangement which the MSP leadership would be prepared to accept, and although the discussions were conducted in a generally constructive atmosphere, neither party has been willing to make the concessions necessary to establish a common administration...`,
return_final:`And so, after receiving the final reports of our representatives and concluding that no further possibility of forming a majority government remains, Mr. Ecevit has personally returned the mandate to the President... It is a particularly disheartening conclusion to multiple gruesome political negotiations, for despite having emerged as the largest party in the general elections and subsequently strengthened our position through an even more impressive performance in the local elections, we have been unable to secure the parliamentary cooperation necessary to form a government. The enthusiasm which had accompanied our initial electoral victory has gradually given way to exhaustion and frustration, while the public, having already endured several years of extraordinary governments following the 12 March Memorandum, must now confront the prospect of yet another prolonged period of political uncertainty...`,
ap_first:`Despite our efforts and the growing popularity of the Left of the Center Movement, the elections have once again ended with the Justice Party emerging as the largest party in the Grand National Assembly... Mr. Demirel, who had already enjoyed two consecutive electoral victories in 1965 and 1969, has managed to secure yet another victory, much to our disappointment. Nevertheless, the results have not been without their consolations, for our party has made considerable progress under its new leadership, and the growing support for our movement gives us much reason to remain optimistic about the future! However, the mandate to form the new government now belongs to AP, and we must watch closely as Mr. Demirel attempts to return to the premiership he was forced to abandon following the Memorandum of 12 March. Whether he will be able to achieve this on his own or find himself dependent on other parties remains to be seen...`,
ap_majority:`The Demirkırat has once again emerged victorious, and this time there is little we can do to prevent its return to government... Having secured enough seats in the National Assembly to govern on its own, the Justice Party has successfully formed a new government under the leadership of Süleyman Demirel, marking his return to the premiership after more than two years of governments established in the aftermath of the 12 March Memorandum. For the third consecutive election, our greatest political rival has defeated us at the ballot box, and despite the progress made by our Left of the Center Movement, the prospect of implementing our ambitious program of political and economic reforms will have to be postponed for another parliamentary term...

Nevertheless, unlike the governments of Erim, Melen and Talu, this new administration enjoys a direct electoral mandate, and the restoration of an elected, single-party government is at least a welcome development for our republic's parliamentary democracy. We shall therefore have to content ourselves with leading the opposition, defending our principles in Parliament and preparing for the next opportunity to bring the CHP back into government. For now, Mr. Demirel has presented his new cabinet to the President of the Republic, with its composition being as follows;`,
ap_cgp_intro:`Although the Justice Party has emerged as the largest party in the elections, its electoral achievements have not been sufficient to secure an outright parliamentary majority. As such, Mr. Demirel must now find himself a coalition partner, and his first choice appears to be none other than the CGP! The two parties are hardly strangers to one another, having already cooperated in supporting Naim Talu's outgoing government, and their shared opposition to our Left of the Center Movement makes a renewed partnership seem rather natural. For our part, it is a particularly unpleasant prospect to see our former comrades potentially helping Demirel return to power, but the arithmetic of Parliament may very well make such an arrangement possible...`,
ap_cgp_talks:`It appears that the political understanding which brought AP and CGP together during the final months of the Memorandum period may now be developing into something considerably more permanent. Reports from Ankara indicate that Mr. Demirel and CGP Chairman Turhan Feyzioğlu have begun discussions over the formation of a new coalition government, with former Prime Minister Ferit Melen and our old colleague Kemal Satır also occupying important positions within CGP's leadership. There is a certain irony in this development, for those who once abandoned our party over fears that the Center of the Left Movement was leading the CHP away from Kemalism are now considering a government under the leadership of the very same Demirel whom we have opposed for so many years... Nevertheless, the two parties have much in common, particularly in their anti-communist convictions and their preference for preserving the existing social and economic order. Their previous cooperation under Talu also offers them an advantage that many other prospective coalitions would lack; the experience of having already worked together in government. From our position in the opposition, we can do little but observe the developments, as the newspapers report on meetings between the two parties and speculation grows over the possibility of a new center-right administration...`,
ap_cgp_formed:`After a series of discussions between the leaderships of the Justice Party and the CGP, an agreement has finally been reached! Mr. Demirel has managed to secure the cooperation of Turhan Feyzioğlu and his colleagues, allowing the two parties to establish a parliamentary majority and form a new coalition government. It is a rather bitter sight for us, watching former members of the CHP enter into government alongside our greatest political rival, particularly when some of these same individuals had once played an important role in shaping our own party's political direction... Nevertheless, the agreement brings an end to the uncertainty surrounding the formation of a government, and the outgoing Talu administration will finally make way for a new cabinet under Demirel's leadership. The details of the negotiations remain largely between the two parties, but the final result is now before the public, as the new AP-CGP government has been announced, with its cabinet being as follows;`,
ap_msp_intro:`With the possibility of a coalition with CGP having failed to produce a government, Mr. Demirel must now consider another possible partner, and this time his attention has turned toward the National Salvation Party... Led by Necmettin Erbakan, the MSP represents a distinctly different political tradition from that of the Justice Party, despite both parties occupying the right of the political spectrum. Indeed, Mr. Erbakan has been an outspoken critic of Demirel's economic policies and has frequently portrayed AP as a party lacking a clear ideological direction, while Demirel himself has hardly been sympathetic toward Erbakan's political movement. Yet politics makes for strange partnerships, and with neither party wishing to see our CHP enter government, perhaps they will discover that their mutual opposition to us offers a more compelling reason for cooperation than their differences offer for remaining apart...`,
ap_msp_talks:`The political developments in Ankara have taken a rather curious turn, as the leaders of the Justice Party and the National Salvation Party have begun discussions over a possible coalition government. Only recently, the two men were engaged in bitter political arguments, with Mr. Erbakan accusing Demirel's economic policies of enriching the wealthy while leaving the poorer sections of society behind, and Demirel questioning the political legitimacy of the movement from which the MSP had emerged. Now, however, the prospect of obtaining power appears to have brought the two parties to the same negotiating table... From what can be gathered through the press, the discussions are unlikely to be particularly straightforward. Although both parties appeal to conservative sections of society, the Justice Party's relatively liberal economic outlook and established relationships with commercial and industrial interests differ considerably from the MSP's emphasis on a nationally directed industrialization campaign and the moral and religious character of society. Moreover, Mr. Erbakan is hardly a man inclined to subordinate his political movement to that of another party without obtaining something substantial in return. Whether Demirel will be willing to accommodate such demands remains uncertain, but as the meetings continue, the possibility of a government uniting the two parties can no longer be dismissed...`,
ap_msp_formed:`Against the expectations of many, including ourselves, the Justice Party and the National Salvation Party have finally reached an agreement on the formation of a coalition government! Whatever differences may have separated Demirel and Erbakan during the election campaign, they have evidently found sufficient common ground to enter government together. For the first time, the National Salvation Party will participate directly in the administration of the republic, an achievement which will undoubtedly strengthen Mr. Erbakan's position within the conservative electorate and give his National Vision Movement an opportunity to influence the direction of government policy. For us, however, the prospect of a coalition between these two parties raises considerable concerns, particularly regarding the future of secularism, economic policy and the social reforms we have long advocated. Still, the negotiations have concluded, and the new government has been presented to the President of the Republic, with Süleyman Demirel returning as Prime Minister and the cabinet of the AP-MSP coalition being as follows;`,
ap_right_intro:`With attempts to establish a government alongside either CGP or MSP having failed, the Justice Party now finds itself in an increasingly difficult position... Mr. Demirel has already exhausted the possibilities of a relatively simple coalition with either party, and yet, rather than abandoning his efforts, he appears determined to bring both of them together in a single government! The prospect of an AP-MSP-CGP coalition is certainly an unusual one, uniting the conservative liberalism of Demirel, the National Vision of Erbakan and the Right-Wing Kemalism of Feyzioğlu under a single administration. These three political movements have considerable disagreements with one another, particularly concerning economic development, religion and the state's relationship with society, but they also share a profound distrust of the political direction our CHP has taken under the Left of the Center Movement. Perhaps their opposition to us will prove sufficient to hold together a government that their individual political philosophies might otherwise make impossible...`,
ap_right_talks:`An increasingly remarkable spectacle is unfolding in Ankara, as the leaderships of three of the country's principal right-wing parties have begun negotiations toward the formation of a common government. Mr. Demirel, having failed to establish a coalition with either CGP or MSP separately, is now attempting to persuade both parties to participate in the same administration, a task which promises to be considerably more difficult than his earlier attempts... Turhan Feyzioğlu and his colleagues in CGP have long presented themselves as defenders of Kemalist principles against what they regard as ideological deviations, particularly those associated with our Center of the Left Movement. Mr. Erbakan and the MSP, on the other hand, have built their political identity around the National Vision, a movement whose understanding of the role of religion in public life is rather difficult to reconcile with the secularist outlook of CGP. As for Demirel, he must somehow bring these competing ideas together while preserving the Justice Party's leadership of the proposed coalition. Newspaper reports speak of continuing discussions between the parties, while political observers speculate over whether a government containing such fundamentally different political tendencies could survive beyond its initial formation. In our party, the atmosphere is one of considerable concern mixed with a certain disbelief; our old colleagues, our greatest political rivals and a movement openly critical of the existing secular order may soon find themselves governing together, united less by a shared program than by their determination to keep the CHP away from power...`,
ap_right_formed:`After prolonged negotiations and considerable uncertainty, Mr. Demirel has finally accomplished what initially appeared to be a rather unlikely feat; the Justice Party, National Salvation Party and Republican Trust Party have reached an agreement to establish a common government! The three parties, despite their conflicting political traditions and disagreements over the direction of the republic, have managed to overcome enough of their differences to present a united parliamentary majority. For the CHP, this development is especially troubling, for the new government brings together much of the parliamentary right under the leadership of our greatest political rival, while leaving the Left of the Center Movement outside the administration of the country. Nevertheless, whether such a coalition will prove capable of governing effectively remains an entirely different question. The differences between Feyzioğlu's Kemalism, Erbakan's National Vision and Demirel's conservatism have not simply disappeared with the signing of a coalition agreement, and the stability of the new government may ultimately depend upon how successfully its members can reconcile their competing ambitions. For the time being, however, a new government has been formed, with Süleyman Demirel assuming the premiership and the AP-MSP-CGP coalition announcing its cabinet as follows;`,
ap_chp_intro:`With all other possibilities for forming a majority government having been exhausted, Mr. Demirel has found himself in a rather peculiar position... Despite emerging victorious in the elections, the Justice Party has been unable to secure the cooperation of the smaller right-wing parties, and the mandate entrusted to its leader now stands in danger of being returned to the President of the Republic. This leaves him with one final possibility; a Grand Coalition between the Justice Party and our CHP, an arrangement that would bring together the two greatest political rivals of the republic under a single government. There is, of course, historical precedent for such an arrangement, for the first coalition government in our republic's history was formed between our CHP and the AP under Ragıp Gümüşpala, following the elections of 1961. However, the political circumstances have changed considerably since then, and the differences between our parties have only grown under the leaderships of Demirel and Ecevit. Nevertheless, the end of the Memorandum governments and the restoration of a stable parliamentary administration remain objectives which both parties can appreciate, and with the parliamentary arithmetic promising an exceptionally strong majority, perhaps an agreement could be found... Mr. Demirel has consequently invited us to enter into discussions over the formation of a coalition government, and despite our considerable reservations, we have agreed to meet him.`,
ap_chp_talks:`It was a rather peculiar day in the Justice Party's Headquarters, the atmosphere was surprisingly familiar, yet the circumstances were anything but... Only a few years ago, we would hardly have imagined ourselves visiting Mr. Demirel to discuss the possibility of serving under his premiership, especially after the considerable political conflicts that had separated our parties during the previous decade. Nevertheless, here we were, sitting across the same men whom we had spent so many years opposing, and this time, to make matters worse, we were the guests rather than the hosts... The smell of freshly prepared Turkish coffee filled the room, while the faces around the table betrayed a rather uncomfortable mixture of exhaustion, caution and restrained politeness. Mr. Ecevit was accompanied by our General Secretary {{secretary}} and Orhan Birgit, both of whom were sitting rather close to him, their attention directed toward the men occupying the opposite side of the table. Ecevit himself maintained a composed expression, though the occasional movement of his moustache and the way he rested his hands upon the table made it apparent that he was not particularly comfortable with the situation.

Across from us sat Mr. Demirel, the victor of yet another general election, though the difficulties he had encountered in forming a government had evidently taken some of the pleasure out of his electoral achievements. He rested comfortably against his chair, occasionally lifting his cup of coffee while listening to the remarks being exchanged, with an expression that suggested he had little intention of allowing us to forget which party had emerged victorious in the elections... To his sides sat AP General Secretary Nizamettin Erkmen and Ahmet Nusret Tuna, both familiar figures from our years of parliamentary opposition. Mr. Erkmen appeared to be the more accommodating of the two on a personal level, although his political loyalty to Demirel made it difficult to imagine him adopting a position significantly different from that of his chairman. Mr. Tuna, on the other hand, appeared somewhat more independent in his demeanor, and although his political views were certainly not close to ours, there was at least the possibility of holding a constructive conversation with him over matters concerning the Constitution and parliamentary democracy.

The initial greetings were polite, though considerably shorter than the usual conventions of political hospitality might have preferred, and it was not long before the conversation turned toward the rather uncomfortable question of why we were sitting together in the first place... The Justice Party had already exhausted its attempts at forming a government with CGP and MSP, while our own party, despite its electoral progress, lacked the parliamentary strength necessary to form an administration of its own. Neither side was particularly enthusiastic about the prospect of a coalition, yet neither could entirely dismiss its advantages. For Demirel, it offered a return to the premiership without having to depend on the smaller parties of the parliamentary right, while for us, it presented the possibility of entering government and implementing at least part of our political program, rather than spending another parliamentary term in opposition. Furthermore, the combined parliamentary strength of our two parties would be sufficient to pursue constitutional amendments, something which might prove particularly useful in reversing some of the political restrictions and institutional damage left behind by the Memorandum period.

However, the first serious disagreement became apparent rather quickly... Unlike the situation which had followed the elections of 1961, when the CHP was the senior partner of a coalition with AP, the parliamentary arithmetic now placed Mr. Demirel in the stronger position. As the leader of the largest party, he made it clear that he expected to retain the premiership, while our party would have to accept the position of junior partner within the proposed coalition. This was certainly not an appealing prospect for Mr. Ecevit, whose efforts to transform the CHP into a genuinely progressive political movement had been directed in no small part against the very political establishment represented by Demirel. Nevertheless, refusing the arrangement outright would mean abandoning what might be our only opportunity to participate in government, and with the alternative being the continuation of the political uncertainty that had already exhausted the country, we could hardly afford to dismiss the proposal without further consideration...

As the discussions continued, it became increasingly apparent that neither side was prepared to abandon its fundamental political identity merely for the sake of establishing a government. Demirel would not willingly accept a coalition in which the Justice Party's electoral victory translated into a subordinate political role, while we could not allow our participation to become little more than an endorsement of AP's existing policies. Yet there remained a narrow possibility of compromise, and with both sides recognizing the advantages of a government commanding such an overwhelming parliamentary majority, the conversation eventually turned toward the practical question of how such an administration might be organized...`,
ap_chp_cabinet:`With the initial discussions concluded, and both parties having recognized the possibility of a Grand Coalition, it was now time to address the question of forming a cabinet. Unlike our previous coalition governments, however, the circumstances were rather uncomfortable for us, for the Justice Party had emerged as the largest party in the elections and Mr. Demirel consequently expected to assume the premiership. Nevertheless, the combined parliamentary strength of our two parties would provide the new administration with a considerable majority, and our own electoral achievements could not simply be disregarded during the allocation of ministries. With Mr. Demirel and his colleagues sitting across us, we now had to determine how many ministries either party would receive, and whether the resulting arrangement would provide our CHP with sufficient influence to justify participating in such an unusual government...`,
ap_chp_powers:`With the cabinet negotiations finally concluded, we must now turn toward what may prove to be the most difficult part of establishing this Grand Coalition; the creation of a common government program... Although the Justice Party and our CHP have managed to find sufficient common ground to consider governing together, our respective political philosophies remain fundamentally different. Mr. Demirel and his colleagues are considerably more conservative in their understanding of social order, while their economic outlook places far greater emphasis on private enterprise and the established commercial and industrial interests of the country. Our own Left of the Center Movement, meanwhile, has committed itself to the democratization of the economy, the expansion of social rights and the pursuit of reforms intended to give the working population a greater role in the political and economic life of the republic. The question of land reform, the organization of labor, the role of the public sector and the future of our social policies will undoubtedly become particular points of contention...

Nevertheless, there are also matters upon which cooperation may prove considerably easier. Both parties have experienced the consequences of military interference in parliamentary politics, and although we have frequently disagreed over the political developments following 12 March, the restoration of civilian political authority and the strengthening of our constitutional democracy remain objectives which we can pursue together. Furthermore, the parliamentary majority available to this coalition may allow us to undertake reforms that neither party could realistically implement on its own. However, such advantages come at a considerable political price, for every concession made to the Justice Party risks disappointing those who placed their faith in our Center of the Left Movement, while every concession made to us will undoubtedly cause dissatisfaction among Demirel's own supporters... The atmosphere is therefore considerably more cautious than optimistic, with neither side entirely trusting the other, yet both recognizing that an agreement could provide our republic with a degree of political stability it has lacked for several years. Whether this uneasy partnership will prove capable of reconciling our differences, or merely postpone an inevitable confrontation between our political movements, remains to be seen...`,
ap_chp_failed:`It is a rather disappointing, though perhaps not entirely unexpected conclusion to our discussions with the Justice Party... Despite the considerable parliamentary strength that a Grand Coalition could have commanded, and the possibility of finally establishing a stable government after years of political uncertainty, the ideological differences and conflicting ambitions of our two parties have ultimately proven too great to overcome. Mr. Demirel was unwilling to make the concessions necessary to accommodate our Left of the Center Movement, while we could hardly accept an arrangement that would require abandoning the very principles upon which our political transformation has been built. The negotiations have therefore ended without an agreement, leaving Mr. Demirel with no successful majority coalition and our CHP with little choice but to prepare for another period in opposition... Yet the government crisis remains unresolved, and with the Justice Party still holding the mandate to form a government, it appears that Demirel may now attempt to establish an administration without securing a parliamentary majority at all...`,
right_external_formed:`After weeks of unsuccessful coalition negotiations, the seemingly endless attempts to establish a government have finally produced an agreement, though not quite the arrangement anyone might have expected... Having failed to form a majority government with either CGP, MSP, or our CHP, Mr. Demirel has returned to the parliamentary right and managed to assemble a minority administration composed of the Justice Party, Republican Reliance Party and National Salvation Party. However, the combined strength of these parties remains insufficient to guarantee a parliamentary majority, and the survival of the new government will therefore depend upon the attitude of an additional political force; the Democratic Party, led by Ferruh Bozbeyli.

There is a certain irony in this development, for Bozbeyli and many of his colleagues had abandoned the Justice Party following the bitter internal disputes of 1970, with their opposition to Demirel contributing directly to the collapse of his parliamentary majority and the formation of a new political party. Now, after years of political rivalry and mutual resentment, the very men who once helped bring down his government may become responsible for keeping his new administration in office... Nevertheless, the Democratic Party has not agreed to enter the coalition itself, instead offering the parliamentary tolerance necessary to allow the proposed minority government to survive its initial confrontation with the National Assembly. The arrangement therefore leaves Mr. Demirel dependent not only upon the cooperation of Erbakan and Feyzioğlu, whose political differences are considerable, but also upon the continued willingness of Bozbeyli's party to refrain from bringing down the administration.

For our CHP, the situation is certainly far from ideal... Not only has the Justice Party managed to return to government despite its repeated failures to establish a majority coalition, but the parliamentary right has now found a means of cooperating without having to resolve many of the disputes that originally divided it. However, whether this arrangement will prove durable remains highly uncertain, for a government dependent upon the tolerance of a party which refuses to participate in its administration can hardly consider its parliamentary position secure. Nevertheless, the political deadlock has finally been broken, and Süleyman Demirel has announced the formation of a new right-wing minority government, supported from outside by the Democratic Party, with its cabinet being as follows;`
};
/* AD-LATER-COALITION-LOCALIZATION-V1 */
Object.assign(text,{
later_chp_first:`The Republican People's Party has won the most seats in the Grand National Assembly! We did very well in the election, and with Mr. Ecevit's Left of the Centre Movement still being popular, there is much excitement at our headquarters. However, winning an election isn't quite the same as forming a government. As we have found out the hard way, we haven't secured an overall majority in Parliament, so we need to form a coalition with one of the other political parties. Although we have done well, will this make them want to work with us?`,
later_chp_cgp_intro:`We did well enough in the election that we can form a coalition with CGP! Despite our past difficulties, grudges, and differing ideologies, we have managed to get CGP to consider the idea of a coalition. Whilst they haven't warmed up to the Left of the Centre Movement as we had hoped, we might just be able to persuade them to return to government with us. We shall begin talks with the CGP leadership.`,
later_chp_cgp_cabinet:`After the initial greetings and discussions between the two parties, we can begin to form our cabinet. Both parties will want to have as much power in the new government as possible. Despite being politically similar, neither party seems keen on giving the other any ministries. So the cabinet will have to be formed according to the number of seats each party holds in Parliament.`,
later_chp_cgp_powers:`Now that the cabinet has been discussed, we need to begin discussing the policies and agenda of our new Kemalist Coalition. Thankfully, CGP is similar to us on many social issues. However, there are some key issues we disagree on, such as economic democratization, our stance on unions, and the Kurdish Question. Despite these differing opinions, the mood is positive. Both parties understand that the other truly wants to improve the republic that our Founding Father created. Whether this will result in a real government program remains to be seen.`,
later_chp_cgp_failed:`Unfortunately, our attempts to form a coalition with CGP have failed, despite us doing well in the election. It appears the disagreements between the two parties have proven too difficult to overcome, and CGP has returned to its headquarters. We will have to look elsewhere to form a government, and the options we have aren't looking good. However, someone needs to run this republic, and we still have our mandate.`,
later_chp_cgp_arithmetic:`Unfortunately, despite our parties being willing to work together, there aren't enough seats to form a government with CGP. Even with all our deputies combined, we still wouldn't have enough to secure a majority. It is disappointing, as CGP would have been one of our most familiar options, but there isn't any point in trying to form a government that won't have the confidence of Parliament. We will have to look at the other parties and hopefully find a better solution.`,
later_chp_choose:`We have already ruled out the possibility of forming a coalition with CGP, so we must now look elsewhere for a political party to form a government with. As you can see, we don't have many great options available to us. The Justice Party is our biggest political rival, the Democratic Party has never been very friendly towards us, and the MSP's National Vision conflicts with many of our core values. However, we have been given the mandate to form a government by the President and can't just give up. We have to pick a political party to try to form a coalition with:`,
later_chp_ap_intro:`We have no other option but to try and form a coalition with Demirkırat. The Justice Party is our biggest political rival, and that certainly doesn't mean we want to work with them. Interestingly enough, the first coalition government in the history of our Republic was formed between our CHP and AP under Ragıp Gümüşpala in 1961. However, our political views are vastly different, and we would need to make a lot of compromises to form a coalition. Still, this would give us a large majority in Parliament and allow us to make some changes to the country that we wouldn't be able to otherwise. Whether Mr. Demirel would allow us to do this is another matter.`,
later_chp_ap_talks:`Today was a pretty tense day at CHP headquarters. We had brought in our representatives to meet Mr. Demirel and his delegation. Mr. Ecevit was sitting with our party representatives and looked very unenthusiastic about the meeting. Mr. Demirel looked satisfied with the whole situation. After the usual small talk, both delegations began to discuss forming a government together. Unsurprisingly, we quickly got into a disagreement about who would have the most influence. Mr. Demirel wasn't keen to put the Justice Party under our leadership, and we weren't keen to give up the mandate. However, we both knew that there weren't many options available to us in Parliament. After much discussion, we decided to move forward with negotiating a cabinet. Whether this awkward alliance would give us a working government remains to be seen.`,
later_chp_ap_cabinet:`Despite our reluctance, we have agreed to continue negotiations. We now need to decide who will have which ministry. Due to our very different ideologies, we quickly realised how important it was to have control over certain ministries. Obviously, Mr. Demirel and his team aren't going to let us run the entire cabinet, and we aren't going to give up the most important tools of government to our biggest political rivals. This is going to be a tough negotiation.`,
later_chp_ap_powers:`We have now decided who will be in charge of which ministry. We now need to decide on our political programme for this rather unusual coalition. Due to our very different views on the economy, social order, and the role of government, we have quite a few disagreements. Our plans for economic democratisation and social reforms don't fit with the Justice Party's desire to keep the economy as it is. Neither of us seems very keen to compromise our principles. However, if we want this coalition to last longer than the negotiations, we will need to create a working government programme that both parties can agree on.`,
later_chp_ap_failed:`As expected, despite the parliamentary arithmetic making a coalition possible, we couldn't come to an agreement. Our differences with AP have proven too great to overcome. Both delegations left feeling quite frustrated. Mr. Demirel and his team have gone back to their headquarters. Our representatives are now wondering if this ever had a chance of working. We have now tried everything to form a coalition, and Mr. Ecevit finds himself in the awkward position of having to return the mandate to the President.`,
later_chp_dp_intro:`We have failed to form a government with our more familiar political partners, so we must now attempt to form an arrangement with the Democratic Party. Led by Ferruh Bozbeyli, DP was formed following the bitter split within the Justice Party. Many of its founding members left Demirel due to disagreements over the direction of their former party. Unfortunately, whilst they don't agree with Demirel, they may not agree with us either. They are very conservative and have very different views from ours. However, we may have an opportunity due to the disagreements between DP and AP. With the parliamentary arithmetic in our favour, we can hopefully persuade Mr. Bozbeyli and his team that working with us would be better than going back under Demirel's influence.`,
later_chp_dp_talks:`Our representatives have finally met Mr. Bozbeyli and the leaders of the Democratic Party. The atmosphere was quite strange, as whilst they definitely aren't friends of our Left of the Centre Movement, they have had disagreements with Mr. Demirel, which means we have something in common. However, the discussions haven't been straightforward. Mr. Bozbeyli and his team are very committed to a conservative political and economic programme. Whilst they may be happy to work with us to stop the Justice Party from returning to power, they are also concerned about what our reforms may look like. We aren't very keen to go back on the promises we made during our election campaign. Despite our disagreements, we have agreed that we should consider the possibility of forming a coalition and have begun negotiations on who will have which ministry.`,
later_chp_dp_cabinet:`We have now finished our initial discussions and need to begin negotiating who will be in charge of which ministry in a potential CHP-DP government. Whilst the Democratic Party has very different political views from ours, its leaders have made it clear that they want enough influence in the new government. Obviously, we aren't going to let a smaller coalition partner tell us what to do in a government formed under Mr. Ecevit's leadership. However, if we want them to work with us, we will have to make some compromises. We need to decide which ministries we are willing to give away.`,
later_chp_dp_powers:`We have now finished our cabinet negotiations and need to decide on our political programme. Whilst we may have come to the table due to our disagreements with Mr. Demirel, that alone isn't enough to form a government. The Democratic Party is very conservative and committed to keeping the economy as it is. Its leaders are obviously not keen to form a coalition that could turn their conservative voters against them. However, we have come this far, and maybe we can come to a satisfactory arrangement if we make the necessary compromises.`,
later_chp_dp_failed:`Unfortunately, we have failed to form a coalition with the Democratic Party. Despite our initial willingness to negotiate, our political differences were too great. Mr. Bozbeyli and his team refused to reach an agreement that would have them supporting policies that run completely against their political programme. We were equally unwilling to compromise the principles of our Left of the Centre Movement. It seems that just disagreeing with Demirel wasn't enough to make us suitable governing partners. Mr. Ecevit must now return the mandate to the President, as we have tried everything.`,
later_chp_msp_intro:`We have tried our more favourable options, so we must now consider forming a government with the National Salvation Party. What an odd idea! To form a coalition between our strictly secular CHP and Mr. Erbakan's Islamist MSP. Whilst we definitely disagree on almost everything relating to religion and government, the National Vision Movement does have some goals similar to ours regarding the economy. We both want to industrialise and make our Republic less reliant on foreign economic interests. However, Mr. Erbakan also wants to support small businesses and the conservative parts of our society. Perhaps, as we have found common ground in the past, there is some hope that we can do so again.`,
later_chp_msp_talks:`Our representatives have begun discussions with the National Salvation Party. Mr. Erbakan and his team have proven to be very unusual negotiating partners. Whilst we clearly have very different ideologies, the atmosphere has been much more positive than expected. Mr. Erbakan stressed how important it was to find common solutions to the problems facing our Republic. He reminded us that any agreement would have to be approved by the authorised bodies of his party. We have tried to highlight where we could find common ground between our political programmes, particularly regarding improving the economy and quality of life for the lower classes. However, neither delegation seems keen to overlook the clear disagreements we have regarding secularism and our social policies. After much discussion, both sides have agreed to move forward with negotiations on who will be in charge of which ministry in a potential government.`,
later_chp_msp_cabinet:`Our initial discussions have now finished, and the representatives have reported back to their leaders. We can now move on to forming a cabinet. Mr. Ecevit has been informed of how our representatives have got on. Mr. Erbakan has also been informed of how his team has done. Whilst the atmosphere has improved slightly, we still need to decide who will have which ministry. Obviously, neither delegation seems keen to make this easy for the other. Due to our very different political programmes, it is very important to have control over certain ministries.`,
later_chp_msp_powers:`We have finished our cabinet negotiations, so we now need to decide on what may be the most challenging aspect of this unusual coalition. We need to agree on a common government programme. Whilst the atmosphere has improved, there is still a large gap between our Left of the Centre Movement and Mr. Erbakan's National Vision. Mr. Erbakan himself has previously described the situation in a suitable way. He explained that whilst different political movements may agree on the illnesses affecting our country, they also need to agree on what is causing them. This is our problem. Whilst both parties realise that we need to address our economic and social issues, our views on why they are happening (and therefore what we need to do to solve them) are very different.`,
later_chp_msp_failed:`As we feared, negotiations between our progressive CHP and the reactionary MSP have failed to produce a governing coalition. Despite the hard work of both delegations, we couldn't overcome our differences. Whether it was who would have which ministry or what our political programme would look like, we couldn't come to an agreement. We are disappointed that we couldn't reach an agreement, but we are also a little annoyed with ourselves for thinking we could. We don't have much time to be frustrated, though, as the country needs a government. Mr. Ecevit must now return the mandate to the President, as we have tried everything.`,
later_chp_return:`After weeks of stressful negotiations, arguments, and increasingly frustrating meetings, Mr. Ecevit has handed the mandate back to the President of the Republic. Despite the fact that we were the largest party in the election, we failed to secure enough parliamentary cooperation to form a government. We are now feeling depressed and frustrated within our HQ, and many of our comrades are asking how we got into this awkward situation again. However, the republic cannot remain without a government for too long, and we have now run out of options, meaning the mandate will pass to the other parties... Unfortunately, this most likely means Mr. Demirel and his team.`,
later_demirel_mandate:`With Mr. Ecevit having handed the mandate back to the President, it is now in the hands of the leader of the Justice Party, Süleyman Demirel. Despite not emerging as the largest party, Demirel seems happy about this, and his team is already discussing the possibility of creating a right-wing majority in Parliament under AP's leadership. Of course, this is not great news for us in the CHP, especially after trying so hard to form a government. However, Demirel has a tough job on his hands, as the rift between the right-wing parties has not gone away. Whether he can get Mr. Erbakan, Mr. Feyzioğlu, and other conservative leaders to work with him remains to be seen.`,
later_ap_msp_talks:`In an interesting twist, the leaders of the Justice Party and National Salvation Party have begun talks about creating a coalition government. Mr. Erbakan has always been very critical of Demirel's economic policies, saying that the Justice Party only looks after the rich and not the poorer members of society. Demirel, meanwhile, has never been too friendly towards the National Vision Movement. However, with the chance of gaining power, they have both come to the negotiating table. Whilst both parties appeal to the conservative members of society, the Justice Party has a fairly liberal economic policy, whilst the MSP has a strong focus on national industrialisation and a moral and religious society. Mr. Erbakan is definitely not going to put his political movement second to another party unless he gets something in return. Whether Demirel will be willing to comply with these demands remains to be seen, but the possibility of creating a government together can no longer be ignored.`,
later_ap_msp_formed:`To everyone's surprise (including ours), the Justice Party and National Salvation Party have come to an agreement to create a coalition government! Despite their differences during the campaign, they have found enough common ground to create a government together. This is great news for Mr. Erbakan, as it gives him another opportunity to bring his National Vision Movement into government and influence the direction of the republic. It is also great news for Demirel, as he has finally managed to get his party back into power despite not winning the election. However, the fact that they are creating a coalition government worries us greatly. The future of secularism, economic policy, and social reform looks bleak. Nevertheless, the negotiations have finished, and the new government has been presented to the President of the Republic. Süleyman Demirel will be Prime Minister, and the cabinet for the AP-MSP government is as follows:`,
later_ap_msp_cgp_talks:`Having failed to create a government with the National Salvation Party, the Justice Party is in a tricky spot. Mr. Demirel has run out of options to create an easy coalition with MSP, and now he seems determined to bring CGP into the negotiations! Creating a coalition government with AP, MSP, and CGP is an unusual move. We have a mix of conservative liberalism from Demirel, National Vision from Erbakan, and Right-Wing Kemalism from Feyzioğlu. Our old comrades from CGP have always claimed that they are the true Kemalists and do not want any deviation from their beliefs. However, Mr. Erbakan's movement has always had a different view on the role of religion. Demirel will have to somehow bring these differing views together while keeping AP in charge of the coalition. We are very worried about this within our HQ. We are in shock that our old comrades, biggest political opponents, and an Islamist movement may soon be running the country, seemingly just because they don't want us in power!`,
later_ap_msp_cgp_formed:`After lots of negotiation and uncertainty, Mr. Demirel has managed to do the seemingly impossible! The Justice Party, National Salvation Party, and Republican Trust Party have come to an agreement to create a joint government! Despite having very different political backgrounds and differing views on where our republic should be heading, they have managed to overcome their differences to create a majority in Parliament. This is very worrying for us, as the new government brings together a large portion of the parliamentary right under the leadership of our biggest political opponent. However, whether this coalition will be able to run the country effectively is another question. The differences between Feyzioğlu's Kemalism, Erbakan's National Vision, and Demirel's conservatism have not gone away. For now, however, Mr. Demirel is once again Prime Minister, and the new AP-MSP-CGP government has announced its cabinet:`,
later_ap_dp_msp_cgp_talks:`It seems Mr. Demirel isn't giving up! After failing to create a majority in Parliament with MSP alone or alongside CGP, he has now turned to the Democratic Party to create a government with nearly all of the right-wing parties! What an interesting mix! Not only do we have Mr. Erbakan and his National Vision, and Mr. Feyzioğlu and his Right-Wing Kemalists, but we also have Mr. Bozbeyli, who left the Justice Party and threatened Demirel's majority in Parliament! It is ironic that those who left AP due to their disagreements with Demirel are now being asked to help bring him back into government. However, there really aren't many options available to him, and so the four parties have begun to discuss creating what will definitely be one of the strangest governments in our republic's history. Whether their dislike of us will be stronger than their disagreements with one another remains to be seen.`,
later_ap_dp_msp_cgp_formed:`Well... It seems Mr. Demirel has finally achieved his ambitious goal! After weeks of negotiation, failure, and disagreement, the Justice Party, Democratic Party, National Salvation Party, and Republican Trust Party have come to an agreement to create a joint government. Despite years of disagreements, differing ideologies, and personal rivalries, the right-wing parties have somehow come together! We are very disappointed within our HQ. We were expecting to have a government led by Mr. Ecevit due to our election win. However, we now have to watch as Demirel returns as Prime Minister with the support of nearly all the main right-wing parties in Parliament. Still, it is hard to see how long this government will last. There are quite a few disagreements between the parties, and trying to please four political movements will definitely be a difficult task. For now, however, the government has been formed, and the cabinet has been announced as follows:`,
later_ap_dp_msp_talks:`As we have failed to create a government, attention has turned back to the right-wing parties. However, this time Mr. Demirel will have to negotiate with parties that have been arguing with each other for years. The Justice Party, Democratic Party, and National Salvation Party have already shown that they are willing to work together in government, and their leaders seem determined to maintain this political alliance. Of course, the election results have changed the balance of power between them, and so questions have arisen over how the ministries will be divided and who will lead the new government. However, it is unsurprising to see these parties attempt to return to government. Despite the issues they faced previously, they are very determined to work together. This is very frustrating for us, as we may now find ourselves in opposition despite being the largest party in the country.`,
later_senturk_mandate:`After Mr. Ecevit's failure to form a government, the President of the Republic has decided to hand the mandate to Şentürk. Having already gained the support of the main parties in the existing right-wing coalition, Şentürk is in a good position to form a government. However, the negotiations have not been easy. Each party, the Justice Party, Democratic Party, and National Salvation Party, has demanded sufficient representation in the cabinet. There are quite a few differences between their political ideologies. However, they have experience working together, which we didn't have. Şentürk will now move forward with forming a new government.`,
later_senturk_formed:`Despite our election win and our efforts to form a government, the right-wing parties have once again managed to hold onto power. After the negotiations between AP, DP, and MSP, Şentürk has successfully formed a new coalition government. This is obviously disappointing within our HQ, as we have once again missed our chance to bring the Left of the Center Movement back into government. However, governing will definitely not be easy for this coalition. There are quite a few disagreements between the parties, and the economic and political issues our republic faces will not disappear. For now, we will have to remain in opposition whilst Şentürk and his team prepare to run the country with the following cabinet:`
});
root.AnatolianCoalitionTexts=text;if(typeof module!=='undefined')module.exports=text;})(globalThis);
